# The root filesystem overlay

Whatever is in this directory is merged into the root filesystem the image is
built with: the *contents* map to that system's root, so `rootfs/etc/inittab`
becomes `/etc/inittab` and replaces what the packages put there. Directories are
merged; files and symlinks overwrite.

It is applied on every rootfs build - a boot-files update included - and after
everything generated from `rootfs.toml`, so a file here is the authority over
anything the build writes.

## What is configured here rather than in rootfs.toml

| File | Is |
|------|-----|
| `etc/inittab` | what runs at boot, and how many consoles get a getty |
| `etc/network/interfaces` | the network: interfaces, addressing, the hook lines |
| `etc/resolv.conf` | the resolver - QEMU's user-mode forwarder at 10.0.2.3 |
| `etc/doas.conf` | who may escalate: `wheel`, no password |

`rootfs.toml` is for the values the build has to interpolate - the hostname, the
accounts and their passwords, the package list, the mirror, the disk layout -
because those have no file to live in. Anything that is a constant belongs here,
where changing it is editing a file rather than a Rust string.

## Adding something

Drop it in: `rootfs/etc/init.d/custom-service` becomes
`/etc/init.d/custom-service` on the image, and the next `cargo xtask rootfs`
copies it in - no reinstall, no package list to change.

A service script has to be executable for OpenRC to start it:

```sh
#!/sbin/openrc-run

depend() {
    need localmount
    after boot
}

start() {
    ebegin "Starting custom service"
    # Your service commands here
    eend $?
}
```
